## The prediction, and the observation

Set the two against each other for the Milky Way. Take the mass enclosed within the Sun's orbit — about 1.05 × 10¹¹ $M_\odot$, from $v = 235$ km/s at 8.2 kpc — and ask what would be observed further out if that were essentially all the mass.

| Radius | Keplerian prediction | Observed |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 8.2 kpc | 235 km/s | 235 km/s |
| 20 kpc | 150 km/s | ~210 km/s |
| 50 kpc | 95 km/s | ~190 km/s |
| 100 kpc | 67 km/s | ~175 km/s |

The observed curve does not fall as predicted. At 50 kpc the discrepancy in speed is a factor of two, which — since mass scales as $v^2$ — is a factor of **four** in enclosed mass. At 100 kpc it is worse.

Converting to enclosed masses:

| Radius | $M(<r)$ |
| --- | --- |
| 8.2 kpc | 1.05 × 10¹¹ $M_\odot$ |
| 20 kpc | 2.05 × 10¹¹ $M_\odot$ |
| 50 kpc | 4.2 × 10¹¹ $M_\odot$ |
| 100 kpc | 7.1 × 10¹¹ $M_\odot$ |

The mass **keeps growing roughly in proportion to radius**, out to distances where the Galaxy contains essentially no stars. That is the content of a flat rotation curve, and it has two immediate mathematical consequences:

**$M(<r) \propto r$.** Constant $v$ in $M = v^2r/G$ means enclosed mass rises linearly with radius.

**$\rho \propto r^{-2}$.** Differentiating a linearly rising mass over a spherical shell gives a density falling as the inverse square of radius — the profile of an **isothermal sphere**, which is roughly what dark-matter halos are observed and simulated to have in their outer regions.

The **mass-to-light ratio** makes the point in the most direct terms. Taking the Galaxy's luminosity as ~3 × 10¹⁰ $L_\odot$:

- Within 8.2 kpc: $M/L \approx 3.5$ — consistent with a normal stellar population, since ordinary stars average a few solar masses per solar luminosity.
- Within 50 kpc: $M/L \approx 14$ — four times higher, with essentially no additional light having been added.

The ratio rises without limit as the radius grows, because mass keeps accumulating while light does not. **The further out one looks, the darker the Galaxy becomes.**

```checkpoint
