a: constant density, ρ ∝ r⁰
x: Constant density gives M ∝ r³ and therefore v ∝ r — solid-body rotation, rising linearly, which is what is seen in the innermost regions of some galaxies but not in the flat outer parts.
a*: ρ ∝ r⁻², the profile of an isothermal sphere
a: ρ ∝ r⁻³, so that the total mass converges
x: A ρ ∝ r⁻³ profile gives M growing only logarithmically, which would produce a declining rotation curve rather than a flat one. It does describe the outermost parts of simulated halos, where the curve does eventually fall.
a: ρ ∝ r⁻¹, the profile found in the innermost regions of simulated halos
x: This is the inner slope of the standard simulated profile, and it gives M ∝ r² and a rising rotation curve — appropriate near the centre, not in the flat region.
hint: Mass in a shell goes as ρ times r². If total enclosed mass is to grow linearly, what must ρ do?
why: Enclosed mass is the integral of ρ over spherical shells of volume 4πr²dr, so M ∝ r requires the integrand ρr² to be constant, giving ρ ∝ r⁻². This is the singular isothermal sphere, the simplest profile producing exactly flat rotation. Real halos deviate at both ends — shallower inside, steeper outside — but over the range where curves are flat, ρ ∝ r⁻² is what the data demand.
