## Tully–Fisher: a relation that should not be so tight

One further observation deepens the puzzle rather than resolving it.

In 1977 Brent Tully and Richard Fisher found that a spiral galaxy's **luminosity** correlates with the **fourth power of its rotation speed**:

$L \propto v_{\text{max}}^{4}$

The relation is remarkably tight — scatter of a few tenths of a magnitude — and it is enormously useful, since measuring a galaxy's rotation speed (from a line width, requiring no spatial resolution) yields its luminosity, and comparing that with its apparent brightness yields its distance. Tully–Fisher is one of the primary rungs of the extragalactic distance ladder.

But consider what it asserts. The rotation speed at large radius is set almost entirely by the **dark halo**, since that is where nearly all the enclosed mass resides. The luminosity is produced entirely by the **stars**, which are a minority component confined to the inner regions. So the relation says that the dark halo's depth predicts the luminous disk's brightness, to within a few tenths of a magnitude, across four orders of magnitude in luminosity.

Why should a halo of invisible material, extending ten times beyond the stars, know so precisely how many stars formed inside it?

Some correlation is expected, since a deeper potential well collects more gas and forms more stars. The difficulty is the relation's **tightness**: galaxy formation involves mergers, feedback, gas loss, and star-formation histories that vary enormously between galaxies, and all of that scatter ought to broaden the relation far more than observed. Explaining the tightness within the standard picture requires a conspiracy of self-regulating processes — plausible in outline, not yet demonstrated in detail. The relation is also, as the next chapter will note, one of the observations that alternative-gravity proposals point to as more naturally explained on their terms.

```checkpoint
q: The Tully–Fisher relation, L ∝ v⁴, is considered puzzling within the standard dark-matter picture because rotation speed at large radius is set by
a: the luminous disk, so the relation is simply a restatement of Newtonian gravity
x: At large radius the enclosed mass is dominated by the halo, not the disk — that is the whole content of a flat rotation curve. If the disk set the speed there would be no puzzle and no dark matter.
a*: the dark halo, while luminosity is produced by the stars — so the relation implies the invisible halo predicts the stellar content inside it to within a few tenths of a magnitude
a: the galaxy's distance, which is what the relation is used to measure
x: This inverts the logic. The relation is *used* as a distance indicator because it links two intrinsic properties; distance is the output, not an input, and it plays no part in why the correlation exists.
a: the central black hole, whose mass correlates with the bulge
x: The black hole holds a few millionths of a galaxy's mass and has no influence on rotation at tens of kiloparsecs. The M–sigma relation it does participate in is a separate scaling law.
hint: Which component dominates the mass where the rotation curve is flat, and which component produces the light?
why: The flat part of a rotation curve is governed almost entirely by the dark halo, while luminosity comes from stars confined to the inner regions — so Tully–Fisher links a property of the invisible component to a property of the visible one. Some correlation is expected, since deeper wells gather more gas, but the observed scatter is only a few tenths of a magnitude across four decades in luminosity, far tighter than the varied merger histories, feedback, and gas loss of galaxy formation would naively allow. Explaining that tightness remains an open problem, and it is among the observations alternative-gravity proposals cite.
```

## What a rotation curve cannot tell you

Two limitations bound what this measurement establishes, and both matter for the next chapter.

**The disk–halo degeneracy.** In the inner regions of a galaxy, the observed rotation can be reproduced either by a massive stellar disk with a modest halo — the "maximum disk" solution — or by a lighter disk within a more concentrated halo. Both fit the curve. Breaking the degeneracy requires independent information about the disk's mass, from stellar population modelling, from the vertical velocity dispersion, or from gravitational lensing. This is why the *inner* mass distributions of galaxies remain contested even where the outer conclusion is secure.

**Rotation curves measure the potential, not its cause.** The measurement establishes that the gravitational field at large radius is far stronger than the visible matter can produce. Whether that is because unseen matter is present, or because gravity behaves differently in the low-acceleration regime, is not determined by the rotation curve alone. Distinguishing those requires observations in which the two hypotheses diverge — which is precisely the next chapter's subject.

Stating this plainly is not hedging. The rotation curve result is secure and enormous: **the visible matter cannot account for the observed motion, by a factor of several, in essentially every spiral galaxy measured.** What it does not do by itself is identify the resolution.

